# The ledger of `tui`: what a full-screen terminal program can do, one block
# per capability, and what Rebel answers. The lists are this machine's,
# 2026-11-18, fetched into the scratch folder and read, never their source:
# Rust's `ratatui` 0.30.2 (the reference, the author's choice of 2026-11-17),
# its crates `ratatui`, `ratatui-core` and `ratatui-widgets` read from
# `cargo doc` (106 types and traits, 178 constants, their methods read from
# each type's page); Go's `github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea` 1.3.10 (74
# functions, 24 types) and `github.com/rivo/tview` 0.42.0 (27 types, 567
# functions and 539 methods), read by `go doc -all`; Python's `textual` 8.2.8
# (its `widgets`, `containers`, `events`, `geometry` and `App`, read by
# `dir()`). The expected values of the tests are what `ratatui` 0.30.2
# answered for the same input, run 2026-11-18 by a program that is not kept; a
# block's `note` says where this package decided otherwise, and why.
# `tview`'s methods are named by the widget they belong to; its setters and
# getters of a colour or a style stand in the block of the style they set.

capability tui/rect  a rectangle of cells, its edges and whether it is empty
  heroes  tui.Rect, tui.is_empty
  go      tview.Box.SetRect, tview.Box.GetRect, tview.Box.GetInnerRect
  rust    ratatui::layout::Rect, Rect::new, Rect::area, Rect::left, Rect::right, Rect::top, Rect::bottom, Rect::is_empty, Rect::as_position, Rect::as_size, ratatui::layout::Position, ratatui::layout::Size, ratatui::layout::Offset
  python  textual.geometry.Region, textual.geometry.Size, textual.geometry.Offset
  note    columns and rows as `i64`, where `ratatui` holds `u16`; a rectangle's cell count is its width times its height, so no function answers it

capability tui/rect-margins  a rectangle shrunk or grown by margins
  heroes  tui.inner, tui.outer
  go      tview.Box.SetBorderPadding
  rust    Rect::inner, Rect::outer, ratatui::layout::Margin
  python  textual.geometry.Region.shrink, textual.geometry.Region.grow, textual.geometry.Spacing
  note    margins that do not fit answer the empty rectangle at 0, 0, as `ratatui` answers

capability tui/rect-meet  two rectangles met, joined, and asked whether they meet
  heroes  tui.intersection, tui.intersects, tui.enclosing
  go      -
  rust    Rect::intersection, Rect::intersects, Rect::union
  python  textual.geometry.Region.intersection, textual.geometry.Region.overlaps, textual.geometry.Region.union
  note    `union` is a word another language keeps as a keyword, so the smallest rectangle holding both is `enclosing`

capability tui/rect-contains  whether a cell is inside a rectangle: where a click landed
