capability tui/layout-split  an area cut into parts side by side or one under another
  heroes  tui.new_layout, tui.split, tui.Layout, tui.Direction
  go      tview.Flex, tview.NewFlex, tview.Flex.AddItem, tview.Flex.SetDirection, tview.Flex.ResizeItem, tview.Flex.RemoveItem, tview.Flex.Clear, tview.Flex.GetItem, tview.Flex.GetItemCount, tview.Flex.SetFullScreen, tview.Grid, tview.NewGrid, tview.Grid.SetRows, tview.Grid.SetColumns, tview.Grid.AddItem, tview.Grid.SetSize, tview.Grid.SetMinSize, tview.Grid.SetOffset, tview.Grid.GetOffset, tview.Grid.RemoveItem, tview.Grid.Clear
  rust    ratatui::layout::Layout, Layout::new, Layout::default, Layout::horizontal, Layout::vertical, Layout::direction, Layout::constraints, Layout::split, Layout::areas, Layout::try_areas, Layout::init_cache, Rect::layout, Rect::layout_vec, Rect::try_layout, ratatui::layout::Direction
  python  textual.containers.Horizontal, textual.containers.Vertical, textual.containers.HorizontalGroup, textual.containers.VerticalGroup, textual.containers.Grid, textual.containers.ItemGrid, textual.containers.Container, textual.layout
  note    `ratatui` places the edges with a linear solver (Cassowary) and rounds each; this package places them by the order its documentation gives and rounds each edge half away from 0, and answers as `ratatui` did on every case of the tests but one: where two parts of one kind must give up space and the split is a half, `ratatui` (`len3 fill1 len3` in 5) answered 2, 0, 3 and this package 3, 0, 2. `tview`'s grid of fixed rows and columns is two layouts, one inside the other

capability tui/layout-constraints  what a part asks: a length, a percentage, a ratio, a minimum, a maximum, a share
  heroes  tui.Constraint
  go      tview.Flex.AddItem (a fixed size or a proportion), tview.Grid.SetRows, tview.Grid.SetColumns
  rust    ratatui::layout::Constraint, Constraint::from_lengths, Constraint::from_percentages, Constraint::from_ratios, Constraint::from_mins, Constraint::from_maxes, Constraint::from_fills
  python  textual's CSS units for width and height (cells, %, fr, auto, min-width, max-width)
  note    the order a part gives up space: a maximum's wish, then a ratio, a percentage, a length, and a minimum last, as `ratatui` answered (`max3 len5` in 6 is 1, 5); `ratatui`'s `from_*` builders are a list of one case, written as a list here

